1
00:00:00,632 --> 00:00:02,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Andrew, I know you do a lot of vibe coding.

2
00:00:03,375 --> 00:00:05,618
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you feel about how it has evolved?

3
00:00:05,678 --> 00:00:13,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And particularly now, in some ways, I see a spirit of we can start with ideas instead of starting necessarily with code or technical specs.

4
00:00:14,645 --> 00:00:17,627
[SPEAKER_02]: But I have very mixed feelings about the term vibe coding.

5
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:18,968
[SPEAKER_02]: I know it's taken off.

6
00:00:19,168 --> 00:00:20,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe the term was stuck with.

7
00:00:20,749 --> 00:00:26,832
[SPEAKER_02]: But I think it's led a lot of people to think, oh, just go with the vibes and accept all the changes in Cursa.

8
00:00:26,853 --> 00:00:28,654
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not like that, right?

9
00:00:29,014 --> 00:00:30,535
[SPEAKER_02]: I think after half a day of...

10
00:00:30,995 --> 00:00:33,717
[SPEAKER_02]: coding of AI, I'm mentally exhausted.

11
00:00:33,817 --> 00:00:35,718
[SPEAKER_02]: So I have very mixed feelings about that term.

12
00:00:35,738 --> 00:00:37,219
[SPEAKER_02]: I tend to call it AI coding these days.

13
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:44,184
[SPEAKER_02]: And maybe I actually have a, you know, listening to, a lot of my teams use lovable v0 and so on.

14
00:00:44,804 --> 00:00:54,747
[SPEAKER_02]: There's actually one interesting thing I've seen, which is among people entering software coding for the first time, I see a little bit of sometimes insecurity, maybe imposter syndrome.

15
00:00:55,187 --> 00:00:56,908
[SPEAKER_02]: I actually have a request to make of all of you.

16
00:00:56,968 --> 00:01:00,049
[SPEAKER_02]: So there's a lesson I learned in the early days of machine learning, which is...

17
00:01:00,809 --> 00:01:09,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Not a single time did I ever see any senior machine learning person go to a junior person and say, the work you did, that's not actually machine learning.

18
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:11,239
[SPEAKER_02]: I have seen that like zero times in my career.

19
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:19,570
[SPEAKER_02]: So what I saw, one of the reasons I think machine learning AI won, why it took off, was because if you want to call yourself a machine learning person, good enough for me.

20
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:20,291
[SPEAKER_02]: You're one of us.

21
00:01:20,491 --> 00:01:25,257
[SPEAKER_02]: And what I hope we can do is when we see someone that's not an official developer,

22
00:01:25,817 --> 00:01:36,597
[SPEAKER_02]: if they're writing code hey if they want to call themselves a developer good enough for me they're they're one of us and i find that is that really embracing welcoming community we go to all of these people that you know

23
00:01:37,077 --> 00:01:39,038
[SPEAKER_02]: did not have a CS degree or whatever.

24
00:01:39,058 --> 00:01:43,040
[SPEAKER_02]: And I actually routinely go to my team and they did something lovable.

25
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:45,982
[SPEAKER_02]: I routinely go and say, congrats, you're now one of us.

26
00:01:46,442 --> 00:01:50,825
[SPEAKER_02]: And I hope we can keep doing that and just embrace everyone to get everyone to code.

